::ENG3_U06_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which slide design best improves audience experience? {
~A full essay paragraph in tiny text.#Incorrect. This overloads the audience.
=A clear title, one relevant visual, and a short evidence phrase.#Correct. This supports understanding without overload.
~Five unrelated images and no claim.#Incorrect. This distracts from the message.
~A blank slide for every point.#Incorrect. Slides should support the presentation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: How should a digital slide support oral delivery? {
~It should contain the full script.#Incorrect. Full scripts on slides overload readers.
~It should contradict the speaker.#Incorrect. Slide and speech should align.
~It should distract from the focus.#Incorrect. Slides should support focus.
=It should highlight key ideas while the speaker explains details aloud.#Correct. Slides support, not replace, the speaker.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which title best supports complex text analysis? {
=Imagery Reveals Character Conflict.#Correct. This title previews the analytical relationship.
~Stuff About the Text.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~Slide 3.#Incorrect. This does not help the audience.
~Pictures and Words.#Incorrect. This does not preview analysis.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: Feedback says the slide has too much text. What revision is best? {
~Add smaller text to fit more information.#Incorrect. This makes reading harder.
~Add more full sentences.#Incorrect. This increases overload.
=Keep key phrases and move details into speaker notes.#Correct. This improves clarity and audience experience.
~Remove the title and keep the paragraph.#Incorrect. The title helps guide the audience.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which visual choice is most audience-centered? {
~A decorative image unrelated to the claim.#Incorrect. Decoration without purpose distracts.
=A simple diagram showing claim, evidence, and explanation.#Correct. This visual clarifies the presentation structure.
~A crowded collage of random photos.#Incorrect. This creates visual clutter.
~An image so small no one can see it.#Incorrect. The audience cannot use it.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: A slide uses yellow text on a white background. What audience-experience issue appears? {
=Low readability.#Correct. Poor contrast makes text hard to read.
~Too much credible evidence.#Incorrect. The issue is readability.
~Strong organization.#Incorrect. Color contrast is not organization.
~Effective paraphrasing.#Incorrect. The issue is visual design.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: A complex analysis slide should help the audience see what relationship? {
~Only the number of slides.#Incorrect. Slide count is not analysis.
~The speaker's favorite colors.#Incorrect. This is decorative.
~Unrelated images and fonts.#Incorrect. These distract.
=The relationship between claim, evidence, and explanation.#Correct. This supports complex analysis.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: A slide title says "Stuff." What revision best improves clarity? {
~Keep "Stuff" because it is short.#Incorrect. Short but vague is not clear.
=Use a title that previews the slide's main point.#Correct. A clear title guides the audience.
~Remove the title completely.#Incorrect. A helpful title supports navigation.
~Add three exclamation marks.#Incorrect. Punctuation does not add clarity.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which slide likely increases cognitive load? {
~One image and three key words.#Incorrect. This is manageable.
=A full paragraph, five images, and tiny source text.#Correct. Too much information overloads the audience.
~A clear title and one chart.#Incorrect. This can support understanding.
~A short quotation with a speaker explanation.#Incorrect. This can be audience-friendly.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: The speaker discusses evidence but the slide shows a decorative beach image. What is the main problem? {
=The visual does not support the oral focus.#Correct. Digital support should match the message.
~The speaker has too much credibility.#Incorrect. The issue is visual relevance.
~The slide title is automatically strong.#Incorrect. The issue is the image.
~The audience has too much source information.#Incorrect. There is no source issue described.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which source note supports audience experience best? {
~A tiny unreadable citation hidden behind an image.#Incorrect. The audience cannot use it.
=A brief readable source note near the evidence.#Correct. This supports credibility without clutter.
~No source information for evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence needs credibility.
~A full bibliography covering the entire slide.#Incorrect. This may overload the slide.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: What is the best revision for a slide with four unrelated images? {
~Add four more images.#Incorrect. This increases clutter.
~Keep all images because visuals are always helpful.#Incorrect. Visuals need purpose.
~Remove the claim and keep the images.#Incorrect. The claim guides the slide.
=Keep the one image that best clarifies the claim.#Correct. This improves focus and audience experience.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which slide text best supports analysis without becoming a script? {
=Claim: Setting mirrors the narrator's uncertainty.#Correct. This is short and analytical.
~In this paragraph, I will explain every detail from my essay in complete sentences on the slide.#Incorrect. This becomes a script.
~There is a story and it has things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~Look at this.#Incorrect. This does not support analysis.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which question should guide digital presentation design? {
~What decoration can I add?#Incorrect. Decoration is not the main goal.
=What does my audience need in order to understand this point?#Correct. Audience need should guide design.
~How can I fit every word on one slide?#Incorrect. That overloads the audience.
~How can I make the slide busier?#Incorrect. Busy slides can reduce understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Why should slide order match oral organization? {
~So the audience gets confused by surprises.#Incorrect. The goal is clarity.
~So visuals can replace all speech.#Incorrect. Slides support speech.
=So listeners can follow the speaker's sequence of ideas.#Correct. Aligned order supports comprehension.
~So the speaker can skip evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: A peer says, "Your slide looks interesting, but I cannot tell the point." What should be revised? {
=Clarify the main claim or title.#Correct. The audience needs the point.
~Add more unrelated design elements.#Incorrect. That worsens confusion.
~Remove all evidence and keep decoration.#Incorrect. Evidence and claim matter.
~Make the text harder to read.#Incorrect. That worsens audience experience.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which multimedia choice supports audience understanding? {
~A loud sound effect after every sentence.#Incorrect. This distracts from meaning.
=A short audio clip only if it directly illustrates the point being analyzed.#Correct. Multimedia should have a clear purpose.
~A video unrelated to the claim.#Incorrect. Unrelated media distracts.
~Animated text that hides the evidence.#Incorrect. Animation should not block meaning.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which slide revision best supports tone and voice appropriate to task? {
~Use slang titles for formal literary analysis.#Incorrect. This may not fit the task.
~Use jokes instead of analysis.#Incorrect. This changes the purpose.
=Use formal, concise slide language that matches the analytical presentation.#Correct. The style fits task and audience.
~Use no title or structure.#Incorrect. This weakens clarity.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: What is the best reason to include an image in a digital presentation? {
~It fills empty space.#Incorrect. Filling space is not enough.
~It looks colorful.#Incorrect. Color alone is not purpose.
~It distracts from a weak claim.#Incorrect. Visuals should not hide weak content.
=It clarifies or reinforces an important idea.#Correct. Images should support understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: A slide has useful evidence but poor organization. Which revision helps most? {
=Group related ideas and place evidence near the claim it supports.#Correct. Organization improves audience understanding.
~Scatter evidence around the slide.#Incorrect. This weakens organization.
~Add unrelated images between evidence and claim.#Incorrect. This distracts.
~Remove the claim and keep only evidence.#Incorrect. The claim is needed.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which slide design best supports a speaker explaining research? {
~A full research paragraph copied onto the slide.#Incorrect. This overloads the audience.
=A short paraphrased source idea with a readable source note.#Correct. This supports evidence and credibility.
~A source title with no explanation or point.#Incorrect. This lacks connection.
~A decorative background that makes text unreadable.#Incorrect. Readability matters.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: What should a speaker do when the slide shows a chart? {
~Assume the audience understands it instantly.#Incorrect. Charts need explanation.
~Ignore the chart and talk about another topic.#Incorrect. The slide should match the speech.
=Explain the chart's key point and connect it to the focus.#Correct. This supports audience experience.
~Read every number without explanation.#Incorrect. This can overload listeners.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which slide choice supports a logical analytical presentation? {
=A sequence of slides moving from claim to evidence to explanation.#Correct. This follows analytical organization.
~Slides in random order.#Incorrect. Random order confuses the audience.
~A closing slide before the claim.#Incorrect. The sequence is backward.
~Evidence slides with no claim.#Incorrect. Evidence needs a guiding claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: Feedback says the slide's wording is too vague. Which revision is best? {
~Replace vague words with more vague words.#Incorrect. That does not help.
~Keep the wording because the speaker understands it.#Incorrect. The audience matters.
=Replace vague wording with precise words that preview the point.#Correct. Precision improves clarity.
~Remove all text and use random images.#Incorrect. This does not clarify the point.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: What is the final check before presenting digital slides? {
=Do the slides improve the audience's understanding of the message?#Correct. This directly checks the standard.
~Are the slides as crowded as possible?#Incorrect. Crowding hurts experience.
~Did I add decoration to every corner?#Incorrect. Decoration is not the goal.
~Can the slides replace all speaking?#Incorrect. Slides support oral presentation.
}
